A||||11||Phil||Alt belt||||1||
Z||000000||Phil||01-09-2000||08:04 PM||||Well it happened again. My sons 88 GT gets about 1500-2000 mi from the alt belt. The pulleys have no burrs, I've put straight edges on the pulleys to check the alignment (as best as I could anyway) I've changed belt brands everything I can think of. When the belt breaks the little ridges on the belt have been worn into sharp vees. I know I have asked for help on this before but thought maybe somebody has had a stroke of smarts since then.||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||01-09-2000||08:51 PM||||I recently noticed during my trials and tribulations with my 2.5L belts that the belts from Autozone (forget the brand name) come with the belt grooves that are sharp or pointed (^^^^^).  Where as most others are more flat with grooves cut in them <BR>( _  _ )<BR>(/ \/ \)<P>Also they are almost 1/3 the price, of course the materials and quality are not near the others that I have been purchasing.  Perhaps this will help you by not solving the problem but learning to suppress the symptoms.  I know that you have checked the alignment, but it sounds as if lateral loading is being applied to the engine (perhaps under load) thus causing the grooves to change cross-section.  Of course this is all theorhetical.  Have you observed the engine under load?  Perhaps something is torquing.  I know this is highly unlikely, but I try to never rule out anything.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000002||Raydar||01-09-2000||10:28 PM||||Phil,<P>Is it possible that something is heating up and siezing(sp?) like the AC compressor? Just an idea...||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||01-09-2000||10:32 PM||||I could be wrong, but I believe the alternator on the v-6 runs on it's own belt.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||01-09-2000||10:36 PM||||Fiero56 you're right||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||01-09-2000||10:52 PM||||Phil, the belts get that way from slipping.  I used to have terrible problems with belt squeal on mine (V-6, no AC), until I realized the oil leaking out of the timing cover seal was contaminating the belt, causing it to slip.  I put a Fel-pro sleeve on the balancer and replaced the seal, and the problem is fixed.  Funny how it started squealing after I replaced my timing chain 8 years ago.  I guess the grooves in the balancer didn't match the new seal.  I use a  Gatorback belt, P/N 4050410, $8 from Summit. ||||reg||3
Z||000006||Phil||01-09-2000||11:09 PM||||The belt stays quiet until just before it breaks. When it starts to make noise we go buy another one and get ready. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||01-09-2000||11:17 PM||||Maybe it is too tight?  My book says this is a possibility, although I don't know how unles you have three hands when you are installing it.  I wish there was a way to put an automatic tensioner on these things.||||reg||5
Z||000008||dogtired||01-10-2000||06:10 AM||||It may be from overtightening??   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>Years ago I purchased a cheep belt tightening tool from JCWhitney. Used it a couple times on other types of cars and broke one end off it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Now I take a 1in socket, put it between the H2O pump pulley and the harmonic bal pulley. Put the broken end of the belt tightener on the socket and the good end on the alt pulley, then tighten with a short crescent wrench, by reaching up through the right wheel well.<BR>I just went through all that, so you can see how I (DO IT) and it isn't all that difficult.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR>I believe I probably overtighten all my belts, even my pants,  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> but haven't gone through many belts, I don't like squeeling belts.<BR>Anyway JCW still sells those belt tighteners, they are cheep quality and cheep in price, about $6.<BR>Haven't tried it on the A/C belt????   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Good Luck!||||reg||6
Z||000009||2birds||01-10-2000||01:00 PM||||Serpentine belts are supposed to have a tension range, but I've always tried to get mine as tight as possible anyway.  They actually make tension gages for it.  I think the concern is side loading on the water pump or alternator bearings.  My friend has a Geo Metro that keeps eating serpentine belts too.  No automatic tensioner there either.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||01-11-2000||08:46 PM||||ignoring crudy belts to start with, 4 things will eat belts.<P>installed too loose<P>installed too tight<P>shot or misaligned pullys.<P>binding bearings in a driven device. (Alt, water pump, etc.)<P>Serpents also hate even the smallest amount of contamination from oil or grease. they are even more cranky about tension than regular V belts.<P>you've already checked some of these. Check them all again anyway. check the driven shafts really carefully. if they are binding at all or sound/feel like a bearing is worn/dry then they could be eating the belts.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Phil||01-11-2000||07:22 PM||||Seeing that's it's cold out now and it is my son's car I think I'll have him do all the checking. <BR>Thanks to all||||reg||1
